little name check for you earlier by uh  michael gove but clearly you're not  completely happy with the  government's policy on kovid 19 what  exactly are you asking for  well sophie businesses across the uk  report that they are frustrated and  angry  they feel like they can't see a clear  strategy to both  manage the pandemic and ensure that the  economy can keep  functioning and the fact is and this is  not discussed enough  every additional restriction that comes  in every  new local lockdown means less demand in  our businesses  more companies forced to shut their  doors and quite simply not enough  support for those who find themselves in  difficult  situations to then trade out thereafter  so it is a grave situation for business  to be in  it does require greater clarity and  greater action on the parts of  government  there's been talk uh about further  national restrictions a  are calling for a two to three week  circuit breaker uh we heard from jeremy  farah who thinks that it would be a good  idea as well member of sage  what would the impact be of more  national restrictions on businesses and  also on jobs  well the impact of either local or  national restrictions  can be severe you've got the firms who  lose all their demand but technically  can  remain open and you have those that are  closed by law  and the impacts there upon individuals  livelihoods upon entire communities  will be felt for a very long time to  come that's why when all of our chamber  leaders across the country  wrote to the prime minister today we  said it was really important to  set out the evidence clearly for all  steps being taken  make sure you're telling businesses  exactly what it is that they need to do  increase the amount of support that you  have on offer  over and above what the treasury has  already put on the table  and really really importantly set out  what an  exit strategy is going to be from these  lockdowns  you've got places like leicester that  have been under a form  of local restrictions since june of this  year  with no prospect of how they then get  out of them  so i have much sympathy with local  leaders around the country who are  saying  if we go into this how do we get back  out again because that's exactly how  many businesses feel too  yeah it does feel as if the restrictions  lasting rather longer than anyone would  have intended  at the beginning i know it's very  difficult but can you quantify in any  way the amount of jobs that are at risk  here  it is impossible to say you you could  you could certainly say that hundreds of  thousands of jobs  are at risk we hear from businesses all  the time who say to us  if i'm closed down again i will not  reopen with the consequences that that  has  for their employees because companies  aren't like  light switches you can't simply switch  them on and off repeatedly  and then go back to normal as if nothing  happened and i think that message needs  to get through more strongly to our  political leaders  both in westminster and in the devolved  administrations as well  simply switching things off for a couple  of weeks and then expecting businesses  to pick the pieces back  up dust themselves off and go back to  normal  isn't the real world and i think that  message needs to get across  when i talk to scientists about the  how you weigh up you know the economic  costs and the  uh the need to suppress the virus they  say that actually  these two things are not different  because if you act swiftly to suppress  the virus  then the economy will be helped  do you think that is right or do you  think that actually  the hard lockdown is clearly having an  economic  impact as well well i think all of us at  the moment are being presented with some  sort of false  choice between public health and the  economy when in fact the two go  so closely together if you don't have an  economy that can function  livelihoods for people etc the outcomes  in terms of lost  income in terms of community deprivation  mental and physical health  are extremely severe as well we should  be looking  at the moments to countries like germany  or new zealand or korea  who have managed to combine public  health measures with good support for  their economies  no secret there those countries have  good mass testing  and they have support for businesses  that rolls out throughout  the calendar year 2021 so businesses  have some certainty as to what's coming  ahead i don't want to make that false  choice i don't want this to be  scientists versus businesses in any way  it's got to be both together because we  need both an economy  and to suppress the virus at the same  time uh now i want to talk  to you about brexit i spoke to michael  gove  earlier on the show who said that he is  now less optimistic that a trade deal  will be struck by the end of the year  number of 10 have said that the talks  are efficiently  pointless if you like unless the eu  moves  their position and compromises more how  concerned are you that we're heading to  a no deal scenario at the end of the  year  well businesses are hugely concerned and  it is not the preferred outcome for the  vast majority  of companies around the country quite  frankly sophie what businesses feel they  are living in is a bit of pantomime  theater between the two sides right now  if as has been reported the two sides  have gotten closer on a number of issue  areas we want them to stop making  declarations to the press  get back into the room around the table  and secure an outcome  that's what's in the mutual interests of  both sides in this discussion  and and rather than convening press  conferences that's  really what we need to see
 

